{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "def make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    rev_string = string[::-1]\n    # Use KMP-like prefix function to find the length of the longest suffix of 'string'\n    # that is also a prefix of 'rev_string'.\n    # This is equivalent to finding the longest palindromic suffix of 'string'.\n    \n    # We construct a combined string: original_string + '#' + reversed_string\n    # The prefix function (LPS array) for this combined string will help find the answer.\n    # The length of the longest proper prefix of the combined string that is also a suffix of the combined string\n    # (represented by pi[-1]) will give the length of the longest palindromic suffix of the original string.\n    combined = string + '#' + rev_string\n    pi = [0] * len(combined)\n    for i in range(1, len(combined)):\n        j = pi[i - 1]\n        while j > 0 and combined[i] != combined[j]:\n            j = pi[j - 1]\n        if combined[i] == combined[j]:\n            j += 1\n        pi[i] = j\n\n    # The length of the longest palindromic suffix of 'string' is pi[-1].\n    # We need to append the prefix of 'rev_string' that is not part of this palindromic suffix.\n    # This prefix is rev_string[:len(string) - pi[-1]].\n    to_append = rev_string[:len(string) - pi[-1]]\n    return string + to_append"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/12", "solution": "def longest(strings: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\" Out of list of strings, return the longest one. Return the first one in case of multiple\n    strings of the same length. Return None in case the input list is empty.\n    >>> longest([])\n\n    >>> longest(['a', 'b', 'c'])\n    'a'\n    >>> longest(['a', 'bb', 'ccc'])\n    'ccc'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not strings:\n        return None\n\n    # Initialize with the first string to handle tie-breaking (first occurrence)\n    longest_so_far = strings[0]\n    max_length = len(strings[0])\n\n    # Iterate through the rest of the strings\n    for s in strings[1:]:\n        current_length = len(s)\n        if current_length > max_length:\n            longest_so_far = s\n            max_length = current_length\n            \n    return longest_so_far"}
